Z||000015||HYPTNOTiSE||06-25-2000||10:46 PM||||I'm using a Split Second ARC-2, pretty sweet unit. I'm shooting for 240hp out of my 3.2.  But it wont be there untill i get some headers made up.  Plus after my build up i'm planning on running ALOT of nitrous. For tuning I use a tech 1.  Goto SplitSec.com for info about the ARC-2. It works by modifing the Map sensors output voltage or something.    <P>Here's why i'm choosing 30lb injectors. I'm shooting for 250 NA hp eventually at crank of course! I know it still seems lofty.  I'm going to say that the Brake Specific Fuel Consumption or BSFC is around .5  to be safe and here's what i do. I take my Target horsepower of 250 and multiply that by .5 or divide it by 2.  i'm at 125 which is the total amount of lbs of fuel per hour that i need and i have only 6 injectors so i divide that by 6 and get 20.8.  Now i'm going to divide that by .8 so i'm getting more safty margin by only running my injectors at an 80% duty cycle. and i'm right at 26lbs per hour. Ford motorsports sells 24lb per hour and 30 lb per hour for around 200 bucks. That's dirt cheap and i though since i can get away with larger injectors why the hell not, that way if i really feel the need i can still up the fuel pressure and turn a few knobs and jug it a little bit. future plans are to add a rotrex and intercool it and run 10lbs of boost and back off the nitrous somewhat. We'll see funds are about scraping rock bottom at this point.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||06-25-2000||11:26 PM||||Have heard of that one. I use a Tech1, Tech 2 and Diacom. All have their place.<P>No doubt you'll eventually gough the asphalt with that setup. Sounds sweet!||||reg||14
Z||000017||HYPTNOTiSE||06-26-2000||12:10 AM||||terry, you seem really knowledgeable and i'm sure that i can learn a lot from you and your experience. I'm 19 years old, and i still have a lot to learn and it's good to meet someone with some real world experience.  What do you do for a living and tell me more about your fiero i'm sure it's BAD ASS!!!<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||06-26-2000||01:00 AM||||Well, it's certainly not typing that's for sure. It should have read "haven't heard.."<P>For food, I'm an engineering manager. Swaps and hacking is for fun. Good on some stuff, I suck on others. Been involved in ECM hacking for a while. Better than some, worse than many.<P>Terry<P>||||reg||12
Z||000019||BBBAD GT||06-26-2000||09:51 AM||||Personally, I wouldn't trust those piggyback A/F meters  that just hook up to your stock O2 sensors.  Even if you have new O2's, the reading you get really aren't nearly as percise as a wide-ban O2 would give.  I would trust an EGT sensor to tell me how Rich/Lean I was going before the stock O2's. <P>I only know this becasue I've been reading my stock O2 sensors for some time now on my 5.0 (using an Interceptor II), and it really only really tells me if I'm generally rich or lean, not how much.  My EGT sensor works pretty good, but of course, its only hooked up to one header tube so far.  <P>My suggestion before you hook up your 30lb injectors and ARM2 is to run the newly rebuilt motor on the same electronics and injectors, and maybe get an adjustable fuel pressure regulator for starters.  Then hook up you big fuel setup.  I've seen way too many people do so many mods at one time, and when something went wrong, they had no idea what it could be (and eventually go crazy and sell everything).  <P>Good luck.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||HYPTNOTiSE||06-26-2000||02:27 PM||||Hey man go back and re read.  I'm using a piggy back computer that goes into the MAP sensor cicuit.  I'm going to have the car tuned on a dyno after setup, and i already have a Holley Adj Fuel Pres REg, an electric fuel pressure gauge, and the shop with the dyno has a Motec meter, which is ever more accurate than a lower priced EGT that measures only one tube, i dont have the money now for a big egt.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||BBBAD GT||06-26-2000||04:11 PM||||My fault, I went to that web site and the first thing I saw what that lil A/F sensor that piggybacks the existing O2's.  I got on a tangent and rambled.....<P>I hope that piggyback computer works for you, seems like an easy enough concept (its been my experieces/observations that tuning a mass air equiped car is sooo much easier than one without it, but I've been mainly limited to Fords tho).  <P>Sounds like you have done tons of work to the V6.  Your estimated 115 mph in the quarter is flat out flying for a non v8 Fiero.  Don't think I've ever heard/seen one do that with a blower or/and N2O before. Also, how did you get those nice wheels to fit (100mm vs 115 mm)?  <P>BTW, whats the difference between a lower priced EGT and a big EGT?   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Sillhouette.com||06-26-2000||04:22 PM||||Hi there!<P>I've been reading these posts about a chip upgrade and found some flaw.  Intially I thought that the 88 ECM does not have a rev limiter so I bought one and sure enough at redline I hit it just like advertised.  So even the 88 has a rev limiter.  If your looking for a computer upgrade your best bet would be to get an 85 GT computer and install it instead of your old computer.  It is a known fact that the 85s are much faster than any other production Fiero including the overated 88.  The reason is that those computer have a knock sensor input that is somehow used in the engine.  Those computers were designed to be used on a turbo engine.  That is why those cars are faster.  So in short don't change your chip change your complete ECM into an 85 one.  Try it it's pretty cool the results.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000023||terryk||06-26-2000||07:29 PM||||The 88 code I've looked at had the limiter set to 655k RPM. The 85 V6 ECM (6869) has the turbo code, wastegate control (but no circuitry loaded to control it), and the ESC code. The 86 and up 7170 ECM has the turbo code, but no way to add the wastegate control circuitry. I believe it still supported ESC.<P>The 86-88 code and calibration tables are very, very close to the 85 code. Both ECM's are the same, just the embedded code in them is a bit different.<P>The ESC was never used on the Fiero V6's, but provisions for it are there.<P>85 ECM and code faster? I don't know about that. I guess the spark might be more agressive. Maybe I'll compare them again.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000024||88formula||06-26-2000||08:38 PM||||I thought the 85 fiero’s were faster because of the 4-speed transmission?||||reg||1
Z||000025||Sillhouette.com||06-26-2000||08:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>The 88 code I've looked at had the limiter set to 655k RPM. The 85 V6 ECM (6869) has the turbo code, wastegate control (but no circuitry loaded to control it), and the ESC code. The 86 and up 7170 ECM has the turbo code, but no way to add the wastegate control circuitry. I believe it still supported ESC.<P>The 86-88 code and calibration tables are very, very close to the 85 code. Both ECM's are the same, just the embedded code in them is a bit different.<P>The ESC was never used on the Fiero V6's, but provisions for it are there.<P>85 ECM and code faster? I don't know about that. I guess the spark might be more agressive. Maybe I'll compare them again.<P>Terry</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Terry I was wondering if you could help me.....<P>I'm trying to find the Revlimiter in the chips for the 86 - 87 V6 where is the Address for this and what is the equation to change it?  <BR>I'm trying to see how much more my engine could rev with the stronger valve springs roller rocker crane cam and forged pistons.  I'm stuck at the standard limit and then it's pulling very hard no float at all just want to see where the engine has it's new limit.  here is my email if you want to send it to me in private  Sillhouette-com@home.com<P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000026||HYPTNOTiSE||06-27-2000||12:22 AM||||Sorry, about saying Big EGT.  I meant a real pricy one with multiple temp sensors and whatnot.  The estimated 115 in the 1/4 seems high to me now, i may have been dreaming or the cartest program may be wrong, but hell it may be right.  The 3.2 i bought is bad ass. It has forged venolia pistons and tapered wrist pins, the rods are all polished.  Balanced real well. I'm also installing an isuzu 5 speed, with 3.83 gears instead of 3.35. which i think  should help my 1/4 out a bunch. 1st and 2nd gears of my 3.83 tranny are almost as low as the 4.10 4 speed, and 3rd and 4th are lower which is the bad part of the 4.10 4 speed, 2-3 shift is to wide of gearing.  Motor should be done in a month, hopefully it'll live up to what i claim  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I'm gonna lay off the nitrous for a while, but when i get the nitrous on, i hope to run in the 12's! that would be kick ass for a v-6 car.  The 18's are a universal 5x100 and 5x115. the supra that they came off is 5x115 and of course the fiero is 5x100, they work really well. And look super sharp, can't wait till i can afford a set of my own, those are my buddys wheels are tires.<BR>||||reg||1
